Understanding the Jenkinsfile

Pipeline-as-Code by diving into Jenkinsfiles — the backbone of Jenkins pipelines.

What is a Jenkinsfile?
A text file (usually stored at the root of your project)
Contains the entire pipeline definition: build, test, deploy steps
Written in Groovy-based Declarative or Scripted syntax
Keeps CI/CD logic version-controlled right alongside your source code

Why It Matters
Pipeline-as-Code: your CI/CD is no longer hidden in Jenkins UI
Traceability & Review: code changes → pull requests → clear history
Repeatability: consistent builds across teams & environments
Portability: clone repo + Jenkinsfile = instantly reproducible pipeline
pipeline {
 agent any
 stages {
  stage('Checkout') { steps { git 'https://lnkd.in/gai2mR4r' } }
  stage('Install') { steps { sh 'npm install' } }
  stage('Test')   { steps { sh 'npm test' } }
  stage('Deploy')  { steps { sh 'pm2 restart app' } }
 }
 post {
  success { echo '✅ Build Succeeded!' }
  failure { echo '❌ Build Failed!' }
 }
}

Key Learnings
Declarative pipelines are clean & readable
Every change in CI/CD goes through Git commits
Easier rollback & audit trails
Freestyle jobs are fine for quick demos, but Jenkinsfiles scale with projects
